Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-79750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that the option\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}\n$$\n\ncannot be written in standard form since the right-hand side depends on $y.$ Since the equation cannot be written in standard form, we conclude that it is not a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations can be written in the standard form, and therefore they are first-order linear ODEs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-79750" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1546f0e9b1bf06bff40e7bfc86b9a5dacb385ae508daadb1ff3ece564edf5238"} {"question" "q-79750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-79750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{y}{2}=x"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x"] ["math" "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+x{y}^{2}=2y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-79750" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy={y}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-79754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-79754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-79754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1546f0e9b1bf06bff40e7bfc86b9a5dacb385ae508daadb1ff3ece564edf5238"} {"question" "q-79754" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-79754" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-y"] ["math" "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}"] ["math" "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-x}{{y}^{2}}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+3x}{y}"] ["math" "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-2x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-79754" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=2$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+4xy=y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=2$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(4x-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1546f0e9b1bf06bff40e7bfc86b9a5dacb385ae508daadb1ff3ece564edf5238"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331738" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-4xy=9$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-4xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-4xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(4x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4xy=9.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $9$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331728" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1546f0e9b1bf06bff40e7bfc86b9a5dacb385ae508daadb1ff3ece564edf5238"} {"question" "q-331728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331728" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=-5y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=-5y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =-5y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy+5y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x-5)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331725" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1546f0e9b1bf06bff40e7bfc86b9a5dacb385ae508daadb1ff3ece564edf5238"} {"question" "q-331725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331725" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1546f0e9b1bf06bff40e7bfc86b9a5dacb385ae508daadb1ff3ece564edf5238"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=y$\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=x$\n- ${y}^{′}+2y=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331742" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1546f0e9b1bf06bff40e7bfc86b9a5dacb385ae508daadb1ff3ece564edf5238"} {"question" "q-331742" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331742" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "q-331742" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/state.json" "file_sha256" "82d26ec01c7dfb334e8f651cca3271269c8f6d609c3cfc997aa82576b9e73b82"} {"question" "e-6599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\ny\\sin x\n-\n\\cos x\n=0\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/example-6599.json" "file_sha256" "b162cdee3aec5248d3227203ea4910cdd414fb8fc7caea5034609eb2d140516f"} {"question" "e-6599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\sin x$ and $Q(x) = \\cos x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/example-6599.json" "file_sha256" "b162cdee3aec5248d3227203ea4910cdd414fb8fc7caea5034609eb2d140516f"} {"question" "e-6600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$$\nx^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\n\\sin(2x)y\n=\ne^x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/example-6600.json" "file_sha256" "2504be715e059feba8a322f1766c943a8b43e34ff362a7cfd2f13a988eed8683"} {"question" "e-6600" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n+\nP(x)y\n=\nQ(x) \\,.\n$$\n\nDividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{2}}y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with $P(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}.$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/example-6600.json" "file_sha256" "2504be715e059feba8a322f1766c943a8b43e34ff362a7cfd2f13a988eed8683"} {"question" "e-21813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n\n- $y' + x^3 y = y$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/example-21813.json" "file_sha256" "7b3e7d259253ad7a897ab7e74e3014bf36bd16e73b0f2c86b9a702c37d402503"} {"question" "e-21813" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = x^4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + x^3 y = y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{3}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{3}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036933/example-21813.json" "file_sha256" "7b3e7d259253ad7a897ab7e74e3014bf36bd16e73b0f2c86b9a702c37d402503"}] "usage" {"e-6599" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283026}] "q-8359" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283029} {"kind" "responses" "entity" 17592186283029}] "e-21813" [] "e-6600" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186283030}]} "basis" 1249 "retractions" [[17592186088364 :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2x - y}{x}$."] [17592186088364 :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne0$, expand the right side as $y^{\\prime}=2-y/x$. Move the $y$ term to the left to obtain $y^{\\prime}+\\frac1x y=2$. This is standard form with $P(x)=1/x$ and $Q(x)=2$."] [17592186087538 :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + y\\sin x - \\cos x =0$"] [17592186087538 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\sin x - \\cos x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + y\\sin x &= \\cos x \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\underbrace{\\sin x}_{P(x)}y &= \\underbrace{\\cos x}_{Q(x)}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\sin x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\cos x\n$$"] [17592186087539 :question/problem "Determine whether the following equation is a first-order linear ODE:\n\n$x^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\sin(2x)y = e^x$."] [17592186087539 :question/worked-solution "We attempt to write the equation in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x) \\,\n$$\n\nOn an interval where $x\\ne0$, dividing the equation through by $x^2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2}\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\sin (2x)y &= e^{x} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{\\sin(2x)}{x^2}y &= \\frac{e^{x}}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that this equation is now in standard form with\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{x^2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{e^x}{x^2}\n$$\n\nBecause the equation can be written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE."] [17592186087390 :question/problem "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n1. $y' + x^3 y = 0$\n2. $y' + x^3 y = x^4$\n3. $y' + x^3 y = y$"] [17592186087390 :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n$y' + x^3 y = 0$.\nNotice that this has the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$. Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n- Next, we consider the equation\n$y' + x^3 y = x^4$.\nNotice that this is written in the form $y' + P(x)y = Q(x)$ where $Q(x) = x^4$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n$y' + x^3 y = y$.\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n$y^{\\prime}+x^3y-y=0$, or $y^{\\prime}+(x^3-1)y=0$.\nSince our equation can be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$, the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186087115 17592186087116 17592186087117 17592186087118 17592186087119] "immutable_fields" [[17592186087114 :answer-field/correct] [17592186087114 :answer-field/id] [17592186087114 :answer-field/key] [17592186087114 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
